  \item Cryptographic integrity under post-quantum adversarial models.
\end{itemize}

\section{Astrophysical Ingestion and CKKS Arithmetic}

Incoming telemetry streams are modeled as real-valued vectors
\(\bm{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n\) derived from multi-messenger sources.
These are embedded into a polynomial ring
\[
  \mathbb{R}[X]/(X^N + 1),
\]
with $N = 2^{16}$ as the polynomial modulus degree.

The CKKS scheme encodes approximate floating-point values into ciphertexts
\(\mathrm{ct} \in \mathcal{C}\) with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Coefficient modulus chain \(Q\) of 438 bits across 6 levels.
  \item Scale factors chosen to balance precision and noise growth.
\end{itemize}

Homomorphic operations are performed via:
\begin{align*}
  \mathrm{ct}{\text{out}} &= \mathrm{Eval}(\mathrm{ct}{\text{in}}) \\
  &= \mathrm{Rescale}\big( \mathrm{NTT}^{-1}(
      \mathrm{NTT}(\mathrm{ct}1) \cdot \mathrm{NTT}(\mathrm{ct}2)
    ) \big),
\end{align*}
where NTT/iNTT denote Number Theoretic Transform operations over RNS limbs.

\section{Bounded-Error Residual Witness Architecture}

Because CKKS is inherently approximate, BAAC-SE v1.0 does not enforce bitwise
equality between decrypted outputs and ideal reference models. Instead, it
introduces an explicit residual polynomial:
\[
  m_{\text{ideal}}(X) \in \mathbb{R}[X]/(X^N+1), \quad
  m'(X) \in \mathbb{R}[X]/(X^N+1),
\]
and defines
\[
  e{\text{error}}(X) = m'(X) - m{\text{ideal}}(X).
\]

Let $e{\text{error}}(X)$ have coefficients $\{ci\}$.
The system enforces an $\ell_\infty$ norm bound:
\[
  |c_i| \le B, \quad \forall i,
\]
where $B$ is the maximum allowable noise bound corresponding to
$\epsilon \le 10^{-12}$ after scaling.

These coefficients are encoded into the zk-SNARK witness, and range-check
gadgets (bit-decomposition and lookup tables) ensure that no coefficient
exceeds $B$.

\section{Groth16 R1CS and QAP Constraints}

The bounded-error architecture is compiled into a Rank-1 Constraint System
(R1CS) compatible with Groth16 over a pairing-friendly curve (e.g., BN254).
Each constraint satisfies:
\[
  \langle Ar, w \rangle \cdot \langle Br, w \rangle
  = \langle C_r, w \rangle,
\]
where $w$ is the witness vector.

Two key constraints are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Residual extraction:}
    \[
      D{\text{coeff}} - I{\text{coeff}} - E_{\text{coeff}} = 0,
    \]
    linking decrypted, ideal, and residual wires.
  \item \textbf{Boolean range-check:}
    \[
      bj (1 - bj) = 0,
    \]
    enforcing bitwise booleanity for each bit $b_j$ in the decomposition of
    $E_{\text{coeff}}$.
\end{itemize}

These R1CS rows are embedded into Quadratic Arithmetic Program (QAP)
polynomials \(A(X), B(X), C(X)\) over an evaluation domain
\(\{t1, \dots, tm\}\), satisfying:
\[
  A(X) B(X) - C(X) = H(X) Z(X),
\]
where \(Z(X)\) is the vanishing polynomial and \(H(X)\) is the prover's
quotient polynomial.

\section{Core-Isolated Real-Time Execution}

BAAC-SE v1.0 is deployed on a Linux host configured with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{isolcpus}, \texttt{nohz\full}, \texttt{rcu\nocbs} kernel parameters.
  \item Dedicated cores for ISR, CKKS pipeline, and Groth16 prover/verifier.
  \item Real-time scheduling (\texttt{SCHED\_FIFO}) and POSIX thread affinity.
\end{itemize}

The execution path is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Interrupt-driven telemetry ingestion on Core 0.
  \item CKKS encryption and evaluation on Core 1.
  \item Groth16 proof generation and verification on Core 2.
  \item Conditional bionic actuation dispatch or fail-safe interlock.
  \item Asynchronous ML-KEM encrypted audit export and Merkle ledger updates.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Post-Quantum Audit and Telemetry Ledger}

